# Terraform
.terraform/
*.tfstate
# Broader than *.tfstate.*backup: also catches terraform.tfstate.1 and the
# numbered copies Terraform leaves behind on a failed state write.
*.tfstate.*
*.tfplan
*.tfvars

# Plan artifacts and crash dumps. `terraform plan -out=FILE` produces a bare
# filename with no extension, so the common names are listed explicitly.
tfplan
plan.out
crash.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# .terraform.lock.hcl is deliberately NOT ignored. Terraform's own guidance is
# to commit it: without it, every `init` is free to resolve different provider
# versions — CI and each operator machine independently — and
# `-lockfile=readonly` becomes impossible, so CI cannot assert that the
# providers it planned with are the ones anyone reviewed.
#
# Force differs by repo, and it is worth being precise. In a DOWNSTREAM the
# lock is load-bearing: that repo is the root module and its lock governs
# every apply. HERE, providers/<cloud>/ is consumed two ways — as a module via
# `source = github.com/...` (in which case the DOWNSTREAM lock wins and this
# one is inert) and run directly per the README quickstart (in which case this
# one is the only pin there is). Committing it covers the second path and
# costs nothing in the first.

# Kubernetes
kubeconfig

